      "text": " It seems there might be some confusion here as adding both a period `.` and a comma `,` together would not make sense within this context since they represent different types of characters—period is used typically at the end of sentences while commas separate items in lists etc. However, if you want to include either periods or commas but not necessarily both simultaneously in your pattern, we can create two distinct regular expressions accordingly. Here’s how it could work based on what was requested beforehand:\n\nFor including only dots OR commas without combining them directly in the same expression due to their differing uses:\n```json\n{\n    \"patterns\": [\n        {\n            \"pattern\": \"^[a-zA-Z0-9,.\\\\s]*$\", // Allows alphanumeric chars along with . and , plus whitespace which includes spaces between words\n            \"flags\": \"\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"pattern\": \"^[a-zA-Z0-9\\\\s]*$\", // Excludes punctuation marks like . and ; from being part of allowed characters except space(s)\n            \"flags\": \"\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\nThe first object allows strings",
